---
import SiteLayout from '../layouts/SiteLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Terminal from '../components/Terminal.astro';
import PageHead from '../components/pages/PageHead.astro';
import PageFoot from '../components/pages/PageFoot.astro';
import McpChat from '../components/McpChat.astro';

const docs = [
  { href: '/docs/getting-started/#coding-agents', label: 'Coding agents in getting started' },
  { href: '/docs/guides/mcp/', label: 'Coding agents and devlanes mcp' },
  {
    href: 'https://github.com/devlanes-run/devlanes/tree/main/agent-plugin',
    label: 'The agent plugin on GitHub'
  },
  { href: '/docs/reference/cli/', label: 'CLI reference' },
  { href: '/docs/security/', label: 'Security' }
];
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Coding agents"
  description="A coding agent starts a lane for its branch with the same devlanes commands you use. The daemon owns the processes, so the agent's shell can end and nothing dies, and the plugin refuses backgrounded dev servers."
  path="/agents/"
  current="agents"
>
  <PageHead title="Give your agent a lane.">
    <p>
      An agent that runs <code>pnpm dev &amp;</code> loses the PID, the logs, and the port the
      moment a second agent does the same on the same machine. With devlanes it starts a lane, gets
      a URL to hand you, and the daemon takes care of the rest.
    </p>
  </PageHead>

  <Section id="daemon" title="No lost PIDs, no port fights, no RAM eaten overnight.">
    <p>
      Two agents on one machine both run the dev server in the background: the second one fails on
      port 3000 and never reads the log that says so. With devlanes each worktree is its own lane,
      the daemon owns the processes, and the agent's shell can end without anything dying. An hour
      later the lane is still there in your own status, with what it costs.
    </p>
    <p>
      The daemon records what each agent session started, so the agent keeps track of nothing:{' '}
      <code>devlanes stop --mine</code> stops only its own units, across every lane, and leaves
      yours alone.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="agent session · ~/dev/myapp-perf" class="terminal-chat">
      <pre class="terminal-content terminal-wrap"><span class="who">you</span>    start the stack and give me the URL

<span class="who">agent</span>  <span class="command">devlanes start</span>
       myapp@3 (agent/perf): 3/3 ready
       <span class="success">ready</span> api    http://127.0.0.1:20030  <span class="url">https://api.myapp3.localhost</span>
       <span class="success">ready</span> web    http://127.0.0.1:20031  <span class="url">https://myapp3.localhost</span>
       <span class="success">ready</span> worker http://127.0.0.1:20032

<span class="who">agent</span>  Your main branch was already running on lane 0, so this
       went up on lane 3: <span class="url">https://myapp3.localhost</span>

<span class="who">you</span>    done, shut it down

<span class="who">agent</span>  <span class="command">devlanes stop --mine</span>
       stopped myapp@3:api
       stopped myapp@3:web
       stopped myapp@3:worker</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="mcp" title="Preview the lane in your own AI app." reverse>
    <p>
      <code>devlanes mcp</code> offers the same commands as MCP tools, for hosts without a shell and
      for sandboxes whose shell cannot reach the daemon. Hosts that show MCP Apps, such as Claude
      Desktop, Copilot in VS Code, Cursor and Goose, render the result as a card with the lane's
      services, their URLs and buttons to open or stop it. Terminal agents get the same text without
      the card.
    </p>
    <p>
      In a terminal, the devlanes skill teaches the agent the commands, and the Claude Code plugin
      refuses a dev server started in the background with <code>&amp;</code>, where nothing
      supervises it and its logs are lost.
    </p>
    <McpChat slot="visual" />
  </Section>

  <Section id="tests" title="Tests run against the lane, then it is put back.">
    <p>
      One command starts the lane, runs the tests with the lane's URLs in their environment, stops
      what it started and exits with the tests' code. The same line runs in CI.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="agent session · ~/dev/myapp-perf">
      <pre class="terminal-content"><span class="dim">agent&gt;</span> devlanes with -- pnpm test:e2e
myapp@3 (agent/perf): 3/3 ready
  42 passed (38.2s)</pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <Section id="box" title="An agent on a box. The URL it prints there opens here." reverse>
    <p>
      Lanes are not a sandbox: every process runs as you. For an agent you want contained, give it a
      VPS, a container or a codespace, run devlanes there, and connect to that box from your laptop.
      The hostname the agent prints is the one you open, with your own certificate.
    </p>
    <Terminal slot="visual" title="on my-vps">
      <pre class="terminal-content"><span class="command">$ devlanes url print web</span>
<span class="url">https://myapp3.localhost</span>
on laptop: <span class="url">https://myapp3.localhost</span></pre>
    </Terminal>
  </Section>

  <PageFoot links={docs} />
</SiteLayout>
